    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> first used the term social democracy in 1844; until 1917 it covered socialists of many kinds, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> it came to mean reformist socialists opposed both to revolution and to the Soviet model.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein">Eduard Bernstein</a>’s revision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> held that socialism could be reached through cooperation across classes and gradual legislative reform; his ideas, unpopular at first, became mainstream after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</li><li>From the 1940s to the 1970s Keynesian economics, agreements between capital and labour and the welfare state defined social democracy in power, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the Nordic countries.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Many of the first social democratic policies were enacted by an opponent: from 1883 the conservative German chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> adopted welfare measures first proposed by the Social Democrats in order to check them, yet by 1912 they were the largest party in parliament.</p></aside>
    </section>

