    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1844</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> coins the term social democracy in his article “The Condition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>”</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1869</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>’s socialism becomes the basis of the Social Democratic Workers’ Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the first social democratic party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1883</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> begins the social programmes that lay the ground for the first modern welfare state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">In the German Revolution the Social Democrats defend what becomes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> against Communists seeking a soviet republic</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1923</span><span class="rf-text">Reformist socialists found the Labour and Socialist International, a rival to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>’ <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1956</span><span class="rf-text">Anthony Crosland’s The Future of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a> argues that traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> has been reformed almost out of existence</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1990s</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tony-blair">Tony Blair</a>’s New Labour revises Clause IV, and the Third Way spreads among social democratic parties</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2013</span><span class="rf-text">Most current and former member parties of the Socialist International found the Progressive Alliance</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-marx.jpg" alt="Karl Marx" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Marx_001_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Jabez Edwin Mayall, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Social democracy grew out of the 19th-century labour movement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> coined the term in 1844, in the second chapter of his article “The Condition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>”, describing the future of the English political system. The tradition drew on both the reformist socialism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-lassalle">Ferdinand Lassalle</a> and the revolutionary socialism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and Engels, and August Bebel, Wilhelm Liebknecht and Carl Wilhelm Tölcke are counted among its founders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. By 1868–1869 Marx’s socialism had become the official basis of the first social democratic party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the Social Democratic Workers’ Party of Germany.</p><p>In the 19th century social democrat was a catch-all name for international socialists who followed Lassalle or Marx, as against the utopian socialists, and it covered revolutionary and non-revolutionary currents alike, though not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a>. Parties of the name included the General German Workers’ Association and the Social Democratic Workers’ Party, which merged to form the Social <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> of Germany, the Social Democratic Federation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party.</p><p>In the early 20th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein">Eduard Bernstein</a>, influenced by the gradualism of the Fabian movement in Britain, rejected orthodox Marxist ideas of the inevitable progress of history and the need for revolution: socialism, he argued, should rest on ethical arguments and come through gradual legislative reform. His party held that reform could only be a means to revolution, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Karl Kautsky</a> attacked him, but Bernstein’s influence grew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In one summary, social democracy rests on three features: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a>, an economy partly regulated by the state, and social support for those in need. It holds that representative democracy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a> is the only acceptable form of government, and it seeks to extend democratic decision-making into the economy through collective bargaining and co-determination rights for workers. It rejects an either/or choice between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and socialism, holding that a progressive evolution of capitalism will gradually turn it into a socialist economy.</p><p>Its common aims are to curb inequality, end the oppression of underprivileged groups, eradicate poverty and provide universally accessible public services such as child care, education, elderly care, health care and workers’ compensation, with income redistributed to pay for them. Social democrats advocate freedom from discrimination by ability, age, ethnicity, gender, language, race, religion, sexual orientation and social class. Where conservatives and liberals also came to accept the welfare state, socialists see in it not merely benefits but the foundation for emancipation and self-determination.</p><p>Bernstein foresaw a long coexistence of democracy with a mixed economy of public, cooperative and private enterprise, with state ownership only where the state could manage best. Attitudes to socialism vary: some social democrats keep it as a long-term goal, others treat it as an ethical ideal to guide reforms within capitalism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-batlle-y-ordonez"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-batlle-y-ordonez.jpg" alt="José Batlle y Ordóñez" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-batlle-y-ordonez">José Batlle y Ordóñez</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JoseBatlle1900.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> divided the movement. Those who supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> renamed themselves Communists, while those opposed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> kept the Social Democrat name. In the German Revolution of 1919 the Communists wanted a soviet republic and the Social Democrats defended what became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, and social democracy went from a “Marxist revolutionary” doctrine to “moderate parliamentary socialism”. The Bolsheviks founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> in 1919, and the reformists answered with the Labour and Socialist International in 1923.</p><p>In the 1920s and 1930s social democracy became dominant in the socialist movement. Under figures such as Carlo Rosselli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, social democrats began to break with orthodox Marxism, embracing liberal socialism and Keynesianism. By the post-war period most European social democrats had abandoned their tie to orthodox Marxism; according to Michael Harrington, this was because they saw Stalin’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as having usurped and distorted its legacy. The Socialist International denounced the communist movement as falsely claiming a share in the socialist tradition. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the Gaitskellites joined a consensus with the Conservatives known as Butskellism, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> social democratic roots appeared under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-batlle-y-ordonez">José Batlle y Ordóñez</a> in the early 20th century.</p><p>In the 1990s many parties adopted the Third Way, an attempt to combine social democratic values with economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, embraced most notably by Bill Clinton’s New Democrats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tony-blair">Tony Blair</a>’s New Labour; some analysts call it neoliberal.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws.jpg" alt="Anti-Socialist Laws" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichsgesetzblatt34_1878.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Social democratic policies were first adopted by an opponent. In the 1880s and 1890s the conservative German chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> put in place welfare measures first proposed by the Social Democrats, after trying to curb them with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a>, and laid the ground for the first modern welfare state. Similar policies followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with the Liberal welfare reforms, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the Progressive Era and Franklin D. Roosevelt’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>, Keynesian and mixed-economy policies spread, and the post-war consensus brought a boom of high growth and full employment until the 1970s. In Britain the Liberal William Beveridge’s report shaped Labour’s National Health Service and welfare state; in 1956 Anthony Crosland claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> had been abolished, a claim Aneurin Bevan disputed. Social corporatism, a partnership of capital and labour bargaining under the state, began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the 1930s and was consolidated in the 1960s and 1970s; from the 1940s to the 1970s Keynesian policy, industrial agreements and the welfare state defined social democracy in power, above all for the Swedish Social Democrats. In the 1980s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a>’s government tried to nationalise all French banks.</p><p>With the crises and stagflation of the 1970s the social democratic welfare state was put in doubt, and public ownership fell below 5% of capital formation in the 1990s. In the Great Recession European social democratic parties increasingly adopted austerity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Other socialists argue that social democracy strengthens the capitalist system it once meant to replace. The democratic socialist philosopher David Schweickart holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> can never be sufficiently humanised and that contradictions suppressed in one place emerge in another: push unemployment too low and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> follows. Marxian socialists argue that welfare policies cannot cure the cycles, exploitation and alienation of capitalism and only legitimise and prolong it, and Jonas Hinnfors argues that in abandoning Marxism social democracy abandoned socialism and became a liberal capitalist movement. Franklin Delano Roosevelt III and David Belkin note that it leaves in place a property-owning class with an interest in reversing its reforms, and John Roemer and Pranab Bardhan that its heavy redistribution depends on a strong labour movement.</p><p>Critics on left and right have argued that reformist socialists in office must govern by capitalist logic. Joseph Schumpeter wrote in Capitalism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a> and Democracy (1942) that socialists would have to “administer” capitalism, and Irving Kristol called democratic socialism “an inherently unstable compound”. Stalin coined the term social fascism for social democracy in the 1930s, a line the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a> adopted.</p><p>Within the movement, opponents of the Third Way have accused figures such as Anthony Giddens of being anti-socialist in practice, while Michael Harrington described a “social democratic compromise” that put human need above the imperatives of profit.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Social democratic ideas have influenced the policies of most Western states since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and in the 21st century the term is often defined by the welfare states and collective bargaining of Northern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, or used as a synonym for the Nordic model. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> World Happiness Report finds the happiest nations concentrated in social democratic countries, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a>, and the Nordic countries rank highest on measures such as economic equality, public health, life expectancy and human development.</p><p>The neoliberal paradigm of the 1980s, accepted by Third Way social democrats too, caused much controversy in the movement, and after the Great Recession the decline known as Pasokification struck many of its parties. Politicians who rejected the Third Way, among them Bernie Sanders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Jeremy Corbyn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, rose in its wake. In the United States, where the legacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> divided the working class and no major socialist party took hold, the Democratic Socialists of America grew to over 120,000 members by July 2026, the largest socialist organisation in American history, and Zohran Mamdani won the 2025 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City mayoral election.</p><p>Today the Social <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the Swedish Social Democratic Party describe their goal as democratic socialism, with social democracy as their principle of action, and most European social democratic parties belong to the Party of European Socialists.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Social democracy is a centre-left ideology that seeks social equality through gradual, democratic reform within a regulated, mostly capitalist economy. Born in the 19th-century labour movement, it parted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> after 1917 and built the welfare states of post-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; since the 1970s it has contended with neoliberalism, the Third Way and electoral decline.</em></p>

